1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
In a tool-heavy agent loop, the fastest thing to blow up your context window isn't the conversation.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,000
It's the raw tool output piling up turn after turn.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:14,000
This lesson covers how to fight that, pruning tool results down to what matters, and

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:19,000
compacting older history when the gist is enough. Let's break it down.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:26,000
Let's define three ways to win back tokens, because the rest of the lesson is really about when to reach for each.

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,000
First, tool output pruning. You trim,

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,000
summarize, or drop verbose tool results, so only the fields you'll actually use downstream survive.

8
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:40,000
This is your primary lever. Second,

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:46,000
context compaction. You periodically compress older turns into a shorter summary and

10
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:51,000
continue from that, which lets a long session run past what full history would allow.

11
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Third, tool result clearing.

12
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
This is a mechanical edit that swaps old tool results for placeholders while leaving your tool calls

13
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:01,000
and reasoning in place.

14
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Same goal, reclaim tokens, but each one makes a different trade-off, and picking wrong costs you.

15
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Let's start with pruning, because in a tool-heavy loop,

16
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:18,000
it's almost always the biggest win. Think about what a single file read or API response

17
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:25,000
actually dumps into your window. It can be tens of thousands of tokens, and most of it is noise

18
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:30,000
you'll never reference again. The pattern is simple. Before that result lands in history for good,

19
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:37,000
cut it down to the load-bearing fields, the values, the status, the one section you needed. Keep

20
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:38,000
those, drop the rest.

21
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:46,000
Because raw tool dumps are the fastest source of bloat, pruning them buys back the most room for the least effort.

22
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:49,000
Reach for this before you reach for anything heavier.

23
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:55,000
Now, the distinction that trips people up, because these two sound alike and aren't,

24
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:01,000
tool result clearing is purely mechanical. The server swaps old tool results for placeholders and

25
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:08,000
leaves your tool calls, your user turns, and your reasoning untouched. There's no model involved,

26
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:15,000
so there's no inference cost. One caveat, editing the prefix invalidates the prompt cache from that point,

27
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,000
so you pay a cache rewrite on the next call.

28
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:24,000
Clear enough at once to make it worthwhile. It's ideal when your bloat is mostly big, refetchable results.

29
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:32,000
Compaction is different. It runs a model to summarize your older context into a compact block, then

30
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:38,000
continues from that summary. Because it's a real inference pass, it costs something, but it can shrink everything.

31
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:43,000
Dialogue and reasoning included, not just tool results.

32
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:47,000
Mechanical and free, or model driven and lossy, that's the choice.

33
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:52,000
Let's get concrete about clearing, because the defaults tell the story.

34
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:58,000
You configure it as a context management edit, and it watches your input token count.

35
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:04,000
By default, it fires when the input crosses a threshold, and it keeps the three most recent tool,

36
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:05,000
use, and result pairs,

37
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:08,000
so your latest work stays intact.

38
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:12,000
Everything older gets its results swapped for a placeholder.

39
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:19,000
You can protect specific tools from ever being cleared, which is how you'd shield something like a memory tool.

40
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:25,000
And because it never touches your tool calls or reasoning, the model still sees that a tool ran,

41
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:29,000
just not the giant payload it returned.

42
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:32,000
Mechanical, targeted, and cheap.

43
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:38,000
Let's look at what actually happens inside a compaction pass, because knowing what survives keeps

44
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:39,000
you out of trouble.

45
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:46,000
When your context approaches the window limit, compaction summarizes the older turns and continues from that summary.

46
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:52,000
What tends to survive is the high-value material, the key facts central to the task,

47
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:59,000
the architectural decisions you've made, and the questions still open. What gets lost is the fine-grained stuff,

48
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:06,000
obscure specifics, and exact verbatim phrasing. And remember, it runs a model to produce that summary,

49
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:07,000
so it isn't free.

50
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:13,000
The takeaway is to lean on compaction when the gist is genuinely enough, and to save anything you'll

51
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:15,000
need word-for-word before it runs.

52
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:21,000
Let's talk defaults and cost, because they shape when compaction kicks in.

53
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:27,000
Compaction watches your input token count and triggers once you cross a configured threshold.

54
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:34,000
The default trigger is 150,000 input tokens, and you can't set it below 50,000.

55
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:39,000
When it fires, the API summarizes the older content and on later requests

56
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:45,000
automatically drops everything before that summary block, so you don't manage the deletion yourself.

57
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:48,000
The cost to keep in mind is the inference pass,

58
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:55,000
summarizing costs, a model call that clearing never needs. So the mental model is straightforward.

59
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:59,000
Clearing is your cheap surgical tool for refetchable results, and

60
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:06,000
compaction is your heavier tool for when the whole history, reasoning included, has grown too large.

61
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:11,000
Three controls do most of the steering on clearing, so they're worth knowing by name.

62
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:17,000
First, keep. That sets how many of your most recent tool use and result pairs stay untouched,

63
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:21,000
so your current work is never the thing that gets wiped.

64
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:23,000
Second, trigger.

65
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:30,000
That's the input token threshold that decides when the edit fires. Cross it and clearing runs to free up room.

66
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:36,000
Third, exclude tools. That's a list of tools whose results are never cleared,

67
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:43,000
which is exactly how you shield something you always want visible. Like a memory tool, keep, trigger, exclude.

68
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:47,000
Three small settings that let you tune clearing to your own loop.

69
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:54,000
One more decision worth making, deliberately. When a session gets huge, compaction isn't your only option.

70
00:05:55,000 --> 00:06:01,000
Sometimes the cleaner move is to start a brand new context window instead of summarizing the old one.

71
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:09,000
Why? Because a capable agent can often rediscover its state from the outside world faster than a summary can carry it,

72
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:14,000
reading the git log, a progress file, or the tests to see where things stand.

73
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:16,000
So the choice isn't automatic.

74
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:22,000
If the running thread genuinely matters, compact it. If the real state lives on disk and the

75
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:23,000
conversation is mostly noise,

76
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:30,000
a fresh window can be simpler and sharper. And whatever you pick, prune tool output along the way,

77
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:32,000
so you never hit overflow.

78
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:35,000
And here's the bottom line.

79
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:40,000
Pruning trims verbose tool outputs down to what matters, and it's your first move in any tool-heavy loop.

80
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:47,000
Just keep in mind that it can be lossy and it's hard to predict what exactly will be lost in the compaction.

81
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:53,000
Clearing is mechanical with no inference cost. Compaction runs a model and is lossy.

82
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:59,000
One thing to watch, save anything you'll need verbatim before compaction summarizes it away.

83
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:04,000
That covers pruning tool output and compaction history. I'll see you in the next one.

